1
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:08,639
The second of the two meetings referred to in the last chapter occurred about a week

2
00:00:08,641 --> 00:00:13,480
after the first. I had again left my boat at the wharf below Bridge;

3
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:16,000
the time was an hour earlier in the afternoon;

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,758
and, undecided where to dine, I had strolled up into Cheapside, and was strolling along it,

5
00:00:21,758 --> 00:00:27,319
surely the most unsettled person in all the busy concourse, when a large hand was laid upon my

6
00:00:27,319 --> 00:00:35,398
shoulder by some one overtaking me. It was Mr. Jaggers’s hand, and he passed it through my arm.

7
00:00:35,398 --> 00:00:42,078
“As we are going in the same direction, Pip, we may walk together. Where are you bound for?”

8
00:00:42,078 --> 00:00:45,319
“For the Temple, I think,” said I.

9
00:00:45,319 --> 00:00:49,199
“Don’t you know?” said Mr. Jaggers.

10
00:00:49,199 --> 00:00:54,480
“Well,” I returned, glad for once to get the better of him in cross-examination,

11
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:58,199
“I do not know, for I have not made up my mind.”

12
00:00:58,199 --> 00:01:04,917
“You are going to dine?” said Mr. Jaggers. “You don’t mind admitting that, I suppose?”

13
00:01:04,917 --> 00:01:08,597
“No,” I returned, “I don’t mind admitting that.”

14
00:01:08,597 --> 00:01:10,438
“And are not engaged?”

15
00:01:10,438 --> 00:01:13,957
“I don’t mind admitting also that I am not engaged.”

16
00:01:13,957 --> 00:01:18,398
“Then,” said Mr. Jaggers, “come and dine with me.”

17
00:01:18,398 --> 00:01:20,756
I was going to excuse myself, when he added,

18
00:01:20,758 --> 00:01:27,000
“Wemmick’s coming.” So I changed my excuse into an acceptance,—the few words I had uttered,

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,116
serving for the beginning of either,—and we went along Cheapside and slanted off to Little Britain,

20
00:01:33,116 --> 00:01:38,116
while the lights were springing up brilliantly in the shop windows, and the street lamp-lighters,

21
00:01:38,116 --> 00:01:42,796
scarcely finding ground enough to plant their ladders on in the midst of the afternoon’s bustle,

22
00:01:42,796 --> 00:01:47,559
were skipping up and down and running in and out, opening more red eyes in the gathering

23
00:01:47,559 --> 00:01:53,641
fog than my rushlight tower at the Hummums had opened white eyes in the ghostly wall.

24
00:01:53,641 --> 00:01:57,879
At the office in Little Britain there was the usual letter-writing, hand-washing,

25
00:01:57,879 --> 00:02:04,078
candle-snuffing, and safe-locking, that closed the business of the day. As I stood idle by

26
00:02:04,078 --> 00:02:10,519
Mr. Jaggers’s fire, its rising and falling flame made the two casts on the shelf look

27
00:02:10,519 --> 00:02:16,480
as if they were playing a diabolical game at bo-peep with me; while the pair of coarse,

28
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:22,160
fat office candles that dimly lighted Mr. Jaggers as he wrote in a corner were

29
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:29,839
decorated with dirty winding-sheets, as if in remembrance of a host of hanged clients.

30
00:02:29,839 --> 00:02:35,398
We went to Gerrard Street, all three together, in a hackney-coach: And, as soon as we got there,

31
00:02:35,398 --> 00:02:39,877
dinner was served. Although I should not have thought of making, in that place,

32
00:02:39,879 --> 00:02:44,519
the most distant reference by so much as a look to Wemmick’s Walworth sentiments,

33
00:02:44,519 --> 00:02:49,718
yet I should have had no objection to catching his eye now and then in a friendly way. But it

34
00:02:49,718 --> 00:02:55,319
was not to be done. He turned his eyes on Mr. Jaggers whenever he raised them from the table,

35
00:02:55,319 --> 00:03:03,038
and was as dry and distant to me as if there were twin Wemmicks, and this was the wrong one.

36
00:03:03,038 --> 00:03:05,641
“Did you send that note of Miss Havisham’s to Mr. Pip,

37
00:03:05,641 --> 00:03:10,078
Wemmick?” Mr. Jaggers asked, soon after we began dinner.

38
00:03:10,078 --> 00:03:14,641
“No, sir,” returned Wemmick; “it was going by post, when you brought Mr. Pip into the

39
00:03:14,641 --> 00:03:20,358
office. Here it is.” He handed it to his principal instead of to me.

40
00:03:20,358 --> 00:03:24,237
“It’s a note of two lines, Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, handing it on,

41
00:03:24,237 --> 00:03:29,758
“sent up to me by Miss Havisham on account of her not being sure of your address. She tells me

42
00:03:29,758 --> 00:03:35,641
that she wants to see you on a little matter of business you mentioned to her. You’ll go down?”

43
00:03:35,641 --> 00:03:41,116
“Yes,” said I, casting my eyes over the note, which was exactly in those terms.

44
00:03:41,116 --> 00:03:43,436
“When do you think of going down?”

45
00:03:43,438 --> 00:03:48,078
“I have an impending engagement,” said I, glancing at Wemmick, who was putting fish

46
00:03:48,078 --> 00:03:55,917
into the post-office, “that renders me rather uncertain of my time. At once, I think.”

47
00:03:55,917 --> 00:04:00,680
“If Mr. Pip has the intention of going at once,” said Wemmick to Mr. Jaggers,

48
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:03,839
“he needn’t write an answer, you know.”

49
00:04:03,839 --> 00:04:07,319
Receiving this as an intimation that it was best not to delay,

50
00:04:07,319 --> 00:04:12,116
I settled that I would go to-morrow, and said so. Wemmick drank a glass of wine,

51
00:04:12,116 --> 00:04:17,078
and looked with a grimly satisfied air at Mr. Jaggers, but not at me.

52
00:04:17,078 --> 00:04:20,879
“So, Pip! Our friend the Spider,” said Mr. Jaggers,

53
00:04:20,879 --> 00:04:24,641
“has played his cards. He has won the pool.”

54
00:04:24,641 --> 00:04:27,078
It was as much as I could do to assent.

55
00:04:27,078 --> 00:04:32,917
“Hah! He is a promising fellow—in his way—but he may not have it all his own way. The stronger will

56
00:04:32,917 --> 00:04:39,680
win in the end, but the stronger has to be found out first. If he should turn to, and beat her—”

57
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:43,519
“Surely,” I interrupted, with a burning face and heart,

58
00:04:43,519 --> 00:04:48,319
“you do not seriously think that he is scoundrel enough for that, Mr. Jaggers?”

59
00:04:48,319 --> 00:04:53,199
“I didn’t say so, Pip. I am putting a case. If he should turn to and beat her,

60
00:04:53,199 --> 00:04:58,078
he may possibly get the strength on his side; if it should be a question of intellect,

61
00:04:58,078 --> 00:05:03,199
he certainly will not. It would be chance work to give an opinion how a fellow of

62
00:05:03,199 --> 00:05:10,000
that sort will turn out in such circumstances, because it’s a toss-up between two results.”

63
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,438
“May I ask what they are?”

64
00:05:12,438 --> 00:05:18,839
“A fellow like our friend the Spider,” answered Mr. Jaggers, “either beats or cringes. He may

65
00:05:18,839 --> 00:05:26,199
cringe and growl, or cringe and not growl; but he either beats or cringes. Ask Wemmick his opinion.”

66
00:05:26,199 --> 00:05:32,557
“Either beats or cringes,” said Wemmick, not at all addressing himself to me.

67
00:05:32,559 --> 00:05:38,358
“So here’s to Mrs. Bentley Drummle,” said Mr. Jaggers, taking a decanter of choicer wine from

68
00:05:38,358 --> 00:05:44,199
his dumb-waiter, and filling for each of us and for himself, “and may the question of supremacy

69
00:05:44,199 --> 00:05:49,000
be settled to the lady’s satisfaction! To the satisfaction of the lady and the gentleman,

70
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:56,879
it never will be. Now, Molly, Molly, Molly, Molly, how slow you are to-day!”

71
00:05:56,879 --> 00:06:01,680
She was at his elbow when he addressed her, putting a dish upon the table. As

72
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:07,879
she withdrew her hands from it, she fell back a step or two, nervously muttering some excuse.

73
00:06:07,879 --> 00:06:13,000
And a certain action of her fingers, as she spoke, arrested my attention.

74
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,519
“What’s the matter?” said Mr. Jaggers.

75
00:06:15,519 --> 00:06:22,319
“Nothing. Only the subject we were speaking of,” said I, “was rather painful to me.”

76
00:06:22,319 --> 00:06:27,436
The action of her fingers was like the action of knitting. She stood looking at her master,

77
00:06:27,438 --> 00:06:31,480
not understanding whether she was free to go, or whether he had more to say to

78
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:36,116
her and would call her back if she did go. Her look was very intent.

79
00:06:37,116 --> 00:06:43,398
Surely, I had seen exactly such eyes and such hands on a memorable occasion very lately!

80
00:06:43,398 --> 00:06:49,199
He dismissed her, and she glided out of the room. But she remained before me as plainly

81
00:06:49,199 --> 00:06:54,879
as if she were still there. I looked at those hands, I looked at those eyes,

82
00:06:54,879 --> 00:07:02,917
I looked at that flowing hair; and I compared them with other hands, other eyes, other hair,

83
00:07:02,917 --> 00:07:08,879
that I knew of, and with what those might be after twenty years of a brutal husband

84
00:07:08,879 --> 00:07:15,597
and a stormy life. I looked again at those hands and eyes of the housekeeper, and thought of the

85
00:07:15,597 --> 00:07:22,319
inexplicable feeling that had come over me when I last walked—not alone—in the ruined garden,

86
00:07:22,319 --> 00:07:28,116
and through the deserted brewery. I thought how the same feeling had come back when I saw a face

87
00:07:28,116 --> 00:07:33,957
looking at me, and a hand waving to me from a stage-coach window; and how it had come back

88
00:07:33,957 --> 00:07:39,480
again and had flashed about me like lightning, when I had passed in a carriage—not alone—through

89
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:45,358
a sudden glare of light in a dark street. I thought how one link of association had helped

90
00:07:45,358 --> 00:07:52,000
that identification in the theatre, and how such a link, wanting before, had been riveted for me now,

91
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,358
when I had passed by a chance swift from Estella’s name to the fingers with their knitting action,

92
00:07:57,358 --> 00:08:04,838
and the attentive eyes. And I felt absolutely certain that this woman was Estella’s mother.

93
00:08:04,839 --> 00:08:09,519
Mr. Jaggers had seen me with Estella, and was not likely to have missed the sentiments I

94
00:08:09,519 --> 00:08:15,000
had been at no pains to conceal. He nodded when I said the subject was painful to me,

95
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,160
clapped me on the back, put round the wine again, and went on with his dinner.

96
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:25,436
Only twice more did the housekeeper reappear, and then her stay in the room was very short,

97
00:08:25,438 --> 00:08:30,358
and Mr. Jaggers was sharp with her. But her hands were Estella’s hands,

98
00:08:30,358 --> 00:08:35,116
and her eyes were Estella’s eyes, and if she had reappeared a hundred times I could

99
00:08:35,116 --> 00:08:39,796
have been neither more sure nor less sure that my conviction was the truth.

100
00:08:39,796 --> 00:08:44,199
It was a dull evening, for Wemmick drew his wine, when it came round,

101
00:08:44,199 --> 00:08:48,237
quite as a matter of business,—just as he might have drawn his salary when that

102
00:08:48,237 --> 00:08:53,639
came round,—and with his eyes on his chief, sat in a state of perpetual readiness for

103
00:08:53,641 --> 00:08:59,957
cross-examination. As to the quantity of wine, his post-office was as indifferent

104
00:08:59,957 --> 00:09:05,358
and ready as any other post-office for its quantity of letters. From my point of view,

105
00:09:05,358 --> 00:09:12,038
he was the wrong twin all the time, and only externally like the Wemmick of Walworth.

106
00:09:12,038 --> 00:09:17,718
We took our leave early, and left together. Even when we were groping among Mr. Jaggers’s stock of

107
00:09:17,718 --> 00:09:23,641
boots for our hats, I felt that the right twin was on his way back; and we had not gone half a

108
00:09:23,641 --> 00:09:28,480
dozen yards down Gerrard Street in the Walworth direction, before I found that I was walking

109
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:35,678
arm in arm with the right twin, and that the wrong twin had evaporated into the evening air.

110
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:42,637
“Well!” said Wemmick, “that’s over! He’s a wonderful man, without his living likeness; but

111
00:09:42,637 --> 00:09:49,758
I feel that I have to screw myself up when I dine with him,—and I dine more comfortably unscrewed.”

112
00:09:49,758 --> 00:09:54,519
I felt that this was a good statement of the case, and told him so.

113
00:09:54,519 --> 00:09:56,597
“Wouldn’t say it to anybody but yourself,” he

114
00:09:56,597 --> 00:10:02,000
answered. “I know that what is said between you and me goes no further.”

115
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,116
I asked him if he had ever seen Miss Havisham’s adopted daughter, Mrs. Bentley Drummle. He said

116
00:10:07,116 --> 00:10:14,116
no. To avoid being too abrupt, I then spoke of the Aged and of Miss Skiffins. He looked

117
00:10:14,116 --> 00:10:18,436
rather sly when I mentioned Miss Skiffins, and stopped in the street to blow his nose,

118
00:10:18,438 --> 00:10:23,957
with a roll of the head, and a flourish not quite free from latent boastfulness.

119
00:10:23,957 --> 00:10:26,277
“Wemmick,” said I, “do you remember telling me,

120
00:10:26,277 --> 00:10:31,879
before I first went to Mr. Jaggers’s private house, to notice that housekeeper?”

121
00:10:31,879 --> 00:10:35,839
“Did I?” he replied. “Ah, I dare say I did.

122
00:10:36,438 --> 00:10:43,559
Deuce take me,” he added, suddenly, “I know I did. I find I am not quite unscrewed yet.”

123
00:10:43,559 --> 00:10:46,277
“A wild beast tamed, you called her.”

124
00:10:46,277 --> 00:10:48,199
“And what do you call her?”

125
00:10:48,199 --> 00:10:52,557
“The same. How did Mr. Jaggers tame her, Wemmick?”

126
00:10:52,559 --> 00:10:57,680
“That’s his secret. She has been with him many a long year.”

127
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:01,597
“I wish you would tell me her story. I feel a particular interest in being

128
00:11:01,597 --> 00:11:05,839
acquainted with it. You know that what is said between you and me goes no further.”

129
00:11:07,078 --> 00:11:13,879
“Well!” Wemmick replied, “I don’t know her story,—that is, I don’t know all of it. But

130
00:11:13,879 --> 00:11:19,796
what I do know I’ll tell you. We are in our private and personal capacities, of course.”

131
00:11:19,796 --> 00:11:21,398
“Of course.”

132
00:11:21,398 --> 00:11:26,635
“A score or so of years ago, that woman was tried at the Old Bailey for murder,

133
00:11:26,637 --> 00:11:31,559
and was acquitted. She was a very handsome young woman, and I believe had some gypsy

134
00:11:31,559 --> 00:11:35,758
blood in her. Anyhow, it was hot enough when it was up, as you may suppose.”

135
00:11:36,839 --> 00:11:39,078
“But she was acquitted.”

136
00:11:39,078 --> 00:11:45,237
“Mr. Jaggers was for her,” pursued Wemmick, with a look full of meaning, “and worked the case in

137
00:11:45,237 --> 00:11:52,160
a way quite astonishing. It was a desperate case, and it was comparatively early days with him then,

138
00:11:52,160 --> 00:11:59,319
and he worked it to general admiration; in fact, it may almost be said to have made him. He worked

139
00:11:59,319 --> 00:12:05,237
it himself at the police-office, day after day for many days, contending against even a committal;

140
00:12:05,237 --> 00:12:11,237
and at the trial where he couldn’t work it himself, sat under counsel, and—every one knew—put

141
00:12:11,237 --> 00:12:18,398
in all the salt and pepper. The murdered person was a woman,—a woman a good ten years older,

142
00:12:18,398 --> 00:12:27,756
very much larger, and very much stronger. It was a case of jealousy. They both led tramping lives,

143
00:12:27,758 --> 00:12:33,358
and this woman in Gerrard Street here had been married very young, over the broomstick (as we

144
00:12:33,358 --> 00:12:41,480
say), to a tramping man, and was a perfect fury in point of jealousy. The murdered woman,—more

145
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:47,237
a match for the man, certainly, in point of years—was found dead in a barn near Hounslow

146
00:12:47,237 --> 00:12:55,199
Heath. There had been a violent struggle, perhaps a fight. She was bruised and scratched and torn,

147
00:12:55,199 --> 00:13:01,557
and had been held by the throat, at last, and choked. Now, there was no reasonable evidence

148
00:13:01,559 --> 00:13:06,758
to implicate any person but this woman, and on the improbabilities of her having been able to do it

149
00:13:06,758 --> 00:13:12,796
Mr. Jaggers principally rested his case. You may be sure,” said Wemmick, touching me on the sleeve,

150
00:13:12,796 --> 00:13:18,758
“that he never dwelt upon the strength of her hands then, though he sometimes does now.”

151
00:13:18,758 --> 00:13:23,796
I had told Wemmick of his showing us her wrists, that day of the dinner party.

152
00:13:23,796 --> 00:13:29,917
“Well, sir!” Wemmick went on; “it happened—happened, don’t you see?—that

153
00:13:29,917 --> 00:13:34,957
this woman was so very artfully dressed from the time of her apprehension, that she looked much

154
00:13:34,957 --> 00:13:41,078
slighter than she really was; in particular, her sleeves are always remembered to have been

155
00:13:41,078 --> 00:13:47,519
so skilfully contrived that her arms had quite a delicate look. She had only a bruise or two

156
00:13:47,519 --> 00:13:54,438
about her,—nothing for a tramp,—but the backs of her hands were lacerated, and the question was,

157
00:13:54,438 --> 00:14:00,199
Was it with finger-nails? Now, Mr. Jaggers showed that she had struggled through a great lot of

158
00:14:00,199 --> 00:14:05,637
brambles which were not as high as her face; but which she could not have got through and kept her

159
00:14:05,637 --> 00:14:11,358
hands out of; and bits of those brambles were actually found in her skin and put in evidence,

160
00:14:11,358 --> 00:14:16,838
as well as the fact that the brambles in question were found on examination to have been broken

161
00:14:16,839 --> 00:14:23,038
through, and to have little shreds of her dress and little spots of blood upon them here and

162
00:14:23,038 --> 00:14:30,000
there. But the boldest point he made was this: it was attempted to be set up, in proof of her

163
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:35,116
jealousy, that she was under strong suspicion of having, at about the time of the murder,

164
00:14:35,116 --> 00:14:42,277
frantically destroyed her child by this man—some three years old—to revenge herself upon him. Mr.

165
00:14:42,277 --> 00:14:49,078
Jaggers worked that in this way: “We say these are not marks of finger-nails, but marks of brambles,

166
00:14:49,078 --> 00:14:54,199
and we show you the brambles. You say they are marks of finger-nails, and you set up

167
00:14:54,199 --> 00:15:01,160
the hypothesis that she destroyed her child. You must accept all consequences of that hypothesis.

168
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:07,519
For anything we know, she may have destroyed her child, and the child in clinging to her may have

169
00:15:07,519 --> 00:15:16,116
scratched her hands. What then? You are not trying her for the murder of her child; why don’t you? As

170
00:15:16,116 --> 00:15:21,758
to this case, if you will have scratches, we say that, for anything we know, you may have accounted

171
00:15:21,758 --> 00:15:27,957
for them, assuming for the sake of argument that you have not invented them?” “To sum up,

172
00:15:27,957 --> 00:15:34,718
sir,” said Wemmick, “Mr. Jaggers was altogether too many for the jury, and they gave in.”

173
00:15:34,718 --> 00:15:37,480
“Has she been in his service ever since?”

174
00:15:37,480 --> 00:15:43,000
“Yes; but not only that,” said Wemmick, “she went into his service immediately after her acquittal,

175
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:48,038
tamed as she is now. She has since been taught one thing and another in

176
00:15:48,038 --> 00:15:52,237
the way of her duties, but she was tamed from the beginning.”

177
00:15:52,237 --> 00:15:55,000
“Do you remember the sex of the child?”

178
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,358
“Said to have been a girl.”

179
00:15:57,358 --> 00:16:00,116
“You have nothing more to say to me to-night?”

180
00:16:00,116 --> 00:16:05,436
“Nothing. I got your letter and destroyed it. Nothing.”

181
00:16:06,038 --> 00:16:11,718
We exchanged a cordial good-night, and I went home, with new matter for my thoughts,

182
00:16:11,718 --> 00:16:13,991
though with no relief from the old. Chapter 49.
